There is <cite id="71d8a709-0">no single universally “best” text watermark</cite>, but the <cite id="71d8a709-2">recent literature emphasizes verifiability and robustness</cite> and the strongest current directions are:

1. <cite id="8fc24c92-1">semantics-based watermark frameworks such as SemaMark, which use semantics instead of tokens to improve robustness against paraphrase</cite>;
2. <cite id="71d8a709-8">semantic sentence-level methods such as SemStamp, which are designed to be robust to sentence-level paraphrase attacks</cite>;
3. <cite id="ae040f38-7">provably robust designs such as Unigram-Watermark, which use a fixed group design to improve robustness against editing and paraphrasing attacks</cite>;
4. and very recent methods like <cite id="2e49750b-3">SEEK, which is explicitly proposed to improve robustness against both scrubbing and spoofing attacks</cite>.

In practice, the <cite id="c0ac31db-2">main advantage of semantic methods is that token-level watermarks are vulnerable to paraphrase attacks because of their token-level design</cite>, while <cite id="71d8a709-8">SemStamp is robust to sentence-level paraphrase attacks</cite> and <cite id="8fc24c92-1">SemaMark improves robustness against paraphrase by leveraging semantics rather than tokens</cite>. <cite id="8fc24c92-4">Provably robust approaches are motivated by the idea that paraphrasing reduces watermark density rather than fully removing it</cite>, and <cite id="ae040f38-7">their reported robustness covers both editing and paraphrasing</cite>. On the newest frontier, <cite id="2e49750b-0">SEEK reports a Pareto improvement by increasing robustness to scrubbing attacks without sacrificing resistance to spoofing</cite>.

But current methods still have important weaknesses. <cite id="5a0b94d8-0">EMNLP 2024 shows that with limited access to model generations, attackers can undo the effects of watermarking and drastically improve paraphrasing attacks</cite>, so <cite id="5a0b94d8-3">reverse-engineering the watermarking scheme must be considered when discussing robustness</cite>. More broadly, <cite id="bfaf73b4-0">robustness evaluations include untargeted attacks like paraphrasing and character modifications, as well as targeted spoofing attacks</cite>, and <cite id="bfaf73b4-2">watermark stealing has been formalized as a threat model connected to spoofing and scrubbing attacks</cite>. Even beyond paraphrase, <cite id="bfaf73b4-6">character-level perturbations are significantly more effective for watermark removal than token-level or sentence-level approaches</cite>. For older token-level schemes especially, <cite id="f9903278-1">token-level sampling watermarking algorithms may not be robust to token-level text edits</cite>.

So the short answer is: <cite id="8fc24c92-1,71d8a709-8,ae040f38-7">the best recent techniques are semantic/paraphrase-robust methods and provably robust edit/paraphrase-robust methods</cite>, and <cite id="2e49750b-3">the newest work also targets the scrubbing-vs-spoofing tradeoff</cite>. They are most robust to <cite id="71d8a709-8,8fc24c92-1">sentence-level paraphrasing and semantic-preserving rewrites</cite> and in some cases <cite id="ae040f38-7">general text editing</cite>. They are still weak against <cite id="5a0b94d8-0,5a0b94d8-3">reverse-engineered paraphrasing attacks</cite>, <cite id="bfaf73b4-6">character-level perturbations</cite>, and <cite id="bfaf73b4-2,bfaf73b4-0">targeted spoofing/stealing attacks</cite>.

