In process analysis for business/event logs, <cite id="123ce82c-7">the field is mature enough to have a 2023 systematic review of anomaly detection for business process event logs</cite>. The <em>leading approach families</em> in that literature are:

- <cite id="7fc82852-9,13393dc1-0">conformance-checking / process-model methods, which compare recorded event logs with standard operating procedures and use traditional trace alignment as a baseline</cite>;
- <cite id="13393dc1-0,7fc82852-5">trace-similarity and profile-based methods, including edit-distance-based and count-based approaches</cite>;
- <cite id="3b1a87cb-0,123ce82c-2">rule-based and statistical methods, such as association-rule mining and information-theoretic online anomaly detection</cite>;
- <cite id="13393dc1-6,3b1a87cb-4,3b1a87cb-1">deep-learning methods, especially neural-network sequence models, autoencoders, variational autoencoders, and transformer autoencoders</cite>;
- and, for newer object-centric settings, <cite id="123ce82c-4,123ce82c-8">graph-based methods using graph neural networks</cite>.

So, the short answer is: <cite id="7fc82852-9,13393dc1-0,3b1a87cb-0,13393dc1-6,123ce82c-4">the main leading approaches are conformance/model-based detection, distance/count-based trace comparison, rule/statistical methods, deep learning, and emerging GNN-based methods for object-centric processes</cite>.

