Not usually in the “re-initialize the offset once per second” sense. In typical GNSS receivers, <cite id="9ec78bf5-7">the receiver first derives an internal reference from its local oscillator by subdividing the oscillator signal to a 1 kHz reference clock</cite>, and <cite id="9ec78bf5-0,51f89cda-1">receiver local time is an estimate of GNSS time based on that local 1 kHz clock</cite>. The navigation engine then estimates <cite id="ff5e58c1-0,ff5e58c1-6">the clock bias as the difference between calculated GNSS system time and receiver local time</cite>.

For timing outputs, the common architecture is that <cite id="6199a03d-1">the receiver controls its own oscillator with the precise time provided by the GNSS receiver to output 1PPS</cite>, often using <cite id="6199a03d-3">a disciplining loop to steer frequency and phase</cite>. A representative timing description is that <cite id="f6ed0ac2-0">if the local 1PPS is late, the oscillator frequency is slightly increased to reduce the phase error</cite>, which indicates continuous steering rather than a hard once-per-second reset. Also, some receivers explicitly state that they can <cite id="51f89cda-7">use the local clock for Timepulse until GPS time is valid, then synchronize Timepulse to GPS</cite>.

So the best short answer is: the PPS is typically generated by digital timing hardware from a local oscillator-derived clock that is disciplined using GNSS time/bias estimates; it is generally <i>steered/retimed continuously</i>, not simply “periodically re-initialized” every second by the PVT clock-bias estimate. \boxed{\text{No—typically PPS is generated from a local oscillator-derived clock and continuously disciplined/phase-steered by GNSS time estimates, not hard re-initialized each second by the PVT clock bias.}}
